#> compiler x86_64-linux-gnu-gcc

#> kernel_image linux/arch/x86/boot/bzImage
#> kernel_export dist/bootx64.efi

#> arch x86_64
#> virt qemu-system-x86_64

#
#       What the compiler is allowed to assume about the machine.
#
#       The kernel was built at -march=x86-64 -mtune=generic, which is the
#       2003 baseline: no SSE4.2, no POPCNT, no CMPXCHG16B, and tuning blended
#       across every x86 ever made including Atom. Nothing could change it --
#       there was no way to pass a C flag to the kernel build at all.
#
#       x86-64-v2 is Nehalem and Bulldozer onward, which is the floor this
#       distribution already claims: any desktop from about 2012. v3 (Haswell,
#       AVX2, BMI) is available and produces better code again, but it would
#       drop Sandy and Ivy Bridge, and those are inside the target.
#
#       Safe despite arch/x86/Makefile disabling SSE for kernel code: -mno-sse
#       and friends are not undone by a later -march. Checked rather than
#       assumed -- compiling with both and disassembling gives zero vector
#       registers at v2 and at v3, and popcnt appears where it should.
#
#       That popcnt is also the floor biting. There are 334 of them in
#       vmlinux, and QEMU's default CPU model -- qemu64, which is SSE3-era --
#       does not have the instruction, so the image dies on it before the
#       console comes up and prints nothing at all. This is why build.sh
#       passes -cpu Nehalem rather than leaving the model to QEMU; booting it
#       by hand needs the same:
#
#           qemu-system-x86_64 -cpu Nehalem ...
#           qemu-system-x86_64 -cpu qemu64,+popcnt,+sse4.1,+sse4.2,+cx16 ...
#
#       both of which were checked against this image. Unlike the Zbb floor
#       profile/arch/riscv declines to set, this one is worth keeping: every
#       machine in the stated range has it, and the loop this is developed in
#       already runs on a model that does.
#
#> kernel_cflags -march=x86-64-v2

#
#       Population count, at the floor the line above sets.
#
#       arch/x86/lib/hweight.S carries a software fallback for processors
#       without POPCNT, reached through an ALTERNATIVE that rewrites all 332
#       call sites to a single popcntq on anything that has it. x86-64-v2
#       includes POPCNT, so that fallback cannot run on a machine this kernel
#       can boot on -- gcc is already emitting the instruction in plain C.
#
#       kernel/replace/hweight.asm is that file with the twiddling replaced by the
#       instruction. Not faster, since the call sites are patched either way;
#       it removes unreachable code and states the floor where it is visible.
#
#> asm hweight.asm arch/x86/lib/hweight.S

#       The spark programs stay at the baseline, and that is a correction
#       rather than an oversight. Raising them to v2 as well looked free --
#       they are ordinary static userspace with no -mno-sse -- and it killed
#       PID 1 on the first boot:
#
#           traps: init[1] trap invalid opcode ip:4006d9
#
#       which disassembles to pinsrq, an SSE4.1 instruction GCC reached for in
#       a structure copy. Real hardware from 2012 has SSE4.1; QEMU's default
#       CPU model does not, so it cost the ability to boot the image in the
#       loop used to develop it, in exchange for better code in programs that
#       are syscall wrappers and were measured spawning in 7.6 us. The kernel
#       is a different case: it is compiled with -mno-sse, so v2 there means
#       integer instructions only and there is no vector code to trip over.
#> program_flags -march=x86-64 -mno-avx -mno-bmi -mno-bmi2 -falign-functions=1
#> program_flags -ffunction-sections


CONFIG_64BIT=y

CONFIG_X86_64=y
# X86_32 is the other architecture and can never be y in this profile.
CONFIG_X86=y
CONFIG_INSTRUCTION_DECODER=y
CONFIG_OUTPUT_FORMAT="elf64-x86-64"
CONFIG_LOCKDEP_SUPPORT=y
CONFIG_STACKTRACE_SUPPORT=y
CONFIG_MMU=y
CONFIG_ARCH_MMAP_RND_BITS_MIN=28
CONFIG_ARCH_MMAP_RND_BITS_MAX=32
CONFIG_ARCH_MMAP_RND_COMPAT_BITS_MIN=8
CONFIG_ARCH_MMAP_RND_COMPAT_BITS_MAX=16
CONFIG_GENERIC_ISA_DMA=y
CONFIG_ARCH_MAY_HAVE_PC_FDC=y
CONFIG_GENERIC_CALIBRATE_DELAY=y
CONFIG_ARCH_HAS_CPU_RELAX=y
CONFIG_ARCH_HIBERNATION_POSSIBLE=y
CONFIG_ARCH_SUSPEND_POSSIBLE=y
CONFIG_AUDIT_ARCH=y
# HAVE_INTEL_TXT is a capability bool selected by INTEL_TXT, not a setting.
# X86_64_SMP no longer exists; SMP alone covers it.
CONFIG_ARCH_SUPPORTS_UPROBES=y
CONFIG_FIX_EARLYCON_MEM=y
CONFIG_PGTABLE_LEVELS=5
# CC_HAS_SANE_STACKPROTECTOR was a compiler capability probe, not a
# setting, and has been removed.

CONFIG_X86_MSR=y
CONFIG_X86_IOPL_IOPERM=y
CONFIG_X86_64_ACPI_NUMA=y
CONFIG_X86_CPUID=y
# X86_X32_ABI and X86_16BIT used to be asserted here. Both default to y,
# so naming them changed nothing except the order of the argument: this
# profile is passed on the build line and lands after prod, which quietly
# undid prod's decision to remove them. Whether they are wanted is policy,
# not architecture, and prod owns it.
CONFIG_X86_FRED=y
# X86_5LEVEL no longer exists: five level paging is decided at runtime.
CONFIG_X86_REROUTE_FOR_BROKEN_BOOT_IRQS=y
CONFIG_X86_MPPARSE=y
CONFIG_X86_CPU_RESCTRL=y
CONFIG_X86_AMD_PLATFORM_DEVICE=y
CONFIG_IA32_EMULATION=y
